I've traced every finding end-to-end. Here are my verdicts.

```json
[
  {
    "findingId": "F1",
    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "reasoning": "Verified mechanically. `CAPTCHA_AAD` is the module-level constant `'mochi-captcha'` (pow.ts:5), used identically at mint (captcha.ts:50) and verify (captcha.ts:66), so every captcha token in an app decrypts at every captcha-protected endpoint — there is no per-endpoint scope anywhere in the sealed payload (`{ iat, nonce, bits }`). verifyCaptcha then honours the sealed `bits` at L111 and the only floor is `options?.minBits !== undefined && bits < options.minBits` at L84-86, which is skipped entirely when the caller omits it. I confirmed the framework knows this matters: Mochi.ts:1786 passes `minBits: protectionOptions.bits` with a comment saying exactly why, and clearance.ts applies the same `bits < minBits` idea to clearance cookies. I grepped the whole repo for `minBits` — it appears only in Mochi.ts:1785-1786 and captcha.test.ts:357-367, and I read all of 150-captcha.md: the Options table lists only bits/minAgeMs/maxAgeMs/store/storePath and the API table shows `verifyCaptcha(formData, options?)` without enumerating options, so `minBits` is genuinely undiscoverable for an app author. The concrete attack holds: an app that mints cheap tokens on one form (`mintCaptcha({ bits: 12 })`, a pattern 152-protection.md:122 itself teaches) and expensive ones on another has its expensive form silently accept the cheap tokens, cutting the intended work by 2^(delta). packages/site/src/routes.ts:261 confirms harvesting is trivial — `/api/captcha-demo/mint` is an unauthenticated JSON endpoint returning a fresh token (rate-limited 60/min). The one thing I'd narrow: the exposure only exists in a mixed-difficulty app (a uniform-`bits` app is unaffected, since all tokens then cost the same), and no identity or authorization boundary is crossed — only proof-of-work cost. I'm keeping MEDIUM because this is precisely the constant-AAD / unscoped-capability-token pattern the project's own threat model asks reviewers to flag, and the fix (default `minBits` to the verifying runtime's `bits`) is a pure improvement."
  },
  {
    "findingId": "F2",
    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "adjustedSeverity": "BUG",
    "reasoning": "The code path is exactly as described. captcha.ts:45 is `const bits = options?.bits ?? resolved.bits;` with no validation — nullish coalescing preserves `0` — and the value is sealed at L50 unchecked. The 1-32 integer bound in config.ts:33-35 guards only the `Mochi.serve({ captcha: { bits } })` path (and, per the comment at config.ts:31, the `captcha:bits` filter, which is applied *before* the bound deliberately); it never sees a per-call mint. verifyCaptcha re-reads the sealed value with only `typeof parsed.bits !== 'number'` (L76) and then evaluates `leadingZeroBits(digest) < bits` at L111, so with `bits: 0` the comparison is `>=0 < 0`, always false, and the proof-of-work check is unconditionally satisfied — including for `pow === ''`, since L65 coerces a missing field to the empty string and `powInput` happily hashes it. I confirmed the asymmetry the finding leans on: `solveBudgetMs`, resolved two lines later, IS validated at L47-49, and captcha.test.ts:150-153 has `test('rejects a non-positive per-mint solve budget')` with no bits counterpart. I also verified the inverted failure mode in the widgets: MochiCaptcha.svelte:116 and MochiCaptchaAuto.svelte:108 both reject `bits < 1` in `validateProps`, mark it non-retryable, and in production `suppressed` collapses the whole `{#if mounted && !suppressed}` block — including the two hidden inputs at L374-375 — to a blank spacer, so real visitors submit no token at all while a scripted post with a token and empty pow passes. The fractional case checks out too: `bits: 0.5` accepts any digest whose first byte is < 128, i.e. ~half of nonces. And `NaN` does fail closed, because `JSON.stringify` writes `null` and the `typeof` guard at L76 rejects it. I'm downgrading to BUG rather than MEDIUM because `bits` is set only by application code — an attacker has no influence over it (the token is AES-256-SIV sealed and unforgeable), so reaching the fail-open requires a developer bug, and that bug simultaneously blocks every legitimate submission, which makes it loud rather than silent. It remains a genuine missing-validation/fail-open gap worth closing in both mintCaptcha and verifyCaptcha."
  },
  {
    "findingId": "F3",
    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "reasoning": "Confirmed in code. `ageMs = Date.now() - iat` at L89 is never clamped, and the floor check at L100 is an unconditional `if (ageMs < minAgeMs) return reject('too-fast', …)`. With `minAgeMs === 0` any negative age is refused, and a zero floor enforces nothing, so the rejection buys no security — a negative age cannot indicate tampering, because `iat` is inside the AEAD-sealed payload and an attacker cannot move it. Mochi.ts:1786 is the framework's own and only `minAgeMs: 0` caller (`verifyCaptcha(formData, { minAgeMs: 0, minBits: protectionOptions.bits })`), so protection mode is exactly where this lands. I traced the client consequence: MochiCaptchaAuto.svelte `submit()` hits `!res.ok` on the 403, calls `failWith(detail)` with the default `retryable = true`, which increments the sessionStorage counter (L89-95) and latches `phase = 'exhausted'` at `maxAttempts` (default 5, resolveProtectionOptions), and `onMount` re-reads the counter at L125 so reloads stay exhausted. The authors' comment at L91-93 argues only against *padding* a positive floor (subtracting an allowance wider than minAgeMs would delete the check) — it does not address the case where there is no floor at all. The strongest evidence this is an oversight rather than a decision is the handled mirror case: clearance.ts:39 returns `ageMs >= -CLEARANCE_DRIFT_ALLOWANCE_MS && ageMs < maxAgeMs`, and protection.test.ts:87-90 has a dedicated test, 'a slightly-future clearance from a fast-clocked instance is accepted'. There is no equivalent test or tolerance for the captcha token. Two caveats keep this at BUG: it needs a multi-instance deploy without sticky routing (or a backward clock step) with skew exceeding the solve time — mint and verify landing on the same instance makes `ageMs` exact — and 150-captcha.md:185 shows the authors expect negative `ageMs` to occur in the field; also 'locked out with no path forward' is a little strong, since the counter is per-tab sessionStorage and a new tab clears it."
  },
  {
    "findingId": "F4",
    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "reasoning": "Straightforwardly confirmed by reading L95-98. The effective floor is `options?.minAgeMs ?? applyFilter('captcha:minAgeMs', resolved.minAgeMs, …)`, so it can come from three sources: the per-call option (which short-circuits the filter entirely — captcha.test.ts:348-353 has a test asserting exactly that bypass), the filter, or the configured default. The throw at L96-98 is unconditional and always blames the filter: `Captcha: the captcha:minAgeMs filter returned ${minAgeMs}`. I verified both misattribution paths concretely. (1) `verifyCaptcha(formData, { minAgeMs: 1_000_000 })` in an app with no filter registered: `limitMs = 900_000 + 30_000 = 930_000`, so `1_000_000 >= limitMs` trips the guard and the operator is told a nonexistent filter misbehaved. (2) A configured `minAgeMs: NaN` slips resolveCaptchaOptions, because its only guard is `minAgeMs >= maxAgeMs` and `NaN >= 900_000` is false; it then fails `Number.isFinite` here, at request time, again naming a phantom filter. The existing test (captcha.test.ts:331-336) only exercises the genuine filter case, which is why the misattribution was never noticed. This is purely a diagnostics/developer-experience defect — no security impact, no behavioural change in the accept/reject decision — so BUG is the right severity, and the finding's own 'high' confidence is justified."
  },
  {
    "findingId": "F5",
    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "adjustedSeverity": "BUG",
    "reasoning": "The algorithmic claim is exactly right: `consume()` walks every entry in `this.seen` (L11-15) before each insert, so one successful verification costs O(n) and sustained traffic is O(n^2); the walk is synchronous JS and blocks the event loop for all other requests; and in the steady state it usually deletes nothing. 'memory' is the default store (config.ts:59), entries live until `iat + maxAgeMs + driftAllowanceMs` (≈15.5 min with defaults, via `expiresAt` at captcha.ts:117), and there is no cap on the map. The authors' justification at L9-10 ('this map only grows one entry per successful send, so the scan stays tiny') is an assumption about volume, not an invariant. The finding's supporting evidence is accurate — I read nonceStore.test.ts:77-89 and it is precisely `test('prunes via an index rather than scanning the table')`, asserting `USING INDEX nonces_expires_at` and `not.toContain('SCAN nonces')` for the SQLite store, i.e. the authors engineered away in SQLite exactly the scan the memory store performs. I'm downgrading from MEDIUM to BUG on exploitability. I traced the insert path in verifyCaptcha: `store.consume` at L118 runs only after minBits, the age floor, the expiry bound and the PoW check all pass, so every entry costs the attacker a genuine 2^bits solve (2^19 by default) plus one mint request and one submit request. That makes the economics backwards for a DoS: the attacker pays tens of milliseconds of CPU per entry, and the server's per-consume walk only approaches that cost once n reaches the millions, which requires roughly a hundred cores sustained for the full 15.5-minute window while simultaneously pushing thousands of requests per second at the app — at which point the flood, not the Map walk, is the problem. The unbounded growth (no ceiling, so memory is attacker-influenced) is arguably the more real half. Worth fixing as the finding recommends (insertion-ordered head-shifting plus a hard cap), but it is a scalability/robustness defect rather than a practical availability vulnerability."
  },
  {
    "findingId": "F6",
    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "reasoning": "Every factual element checks out. The constructor (L27-33) is `new Database(path, { create: true })` followed only by two DDL statements — no pragmas at all. I confirmed against Bun's own documentation (bun.com/docs/runtime/sqlite, 'WAL mode') that WAL is not the default and must be enabled with `db.run(\"PRAGMA journal_mode = WAL;\")`, and found no documented default `busy_timeout` anywhere in the Bun docs; SQLite's own default is no busy handler, so a losing writer gets SQLITE_BUSY immediately. Meanwhile `consume()` takes two separate implicit write transactions on every call — an unconditional `DELETE … WHERE expires_at < ?` at L39 then the `INSERT OR IGNORE` at L40 — and in rollback-journal mode each commit fsyncs under an EXCLUSIVE whole-database lock. The configuration this breaks is the one the docs prescribe: 150-captcha.md:195 says 'Use `store: 'sqlite'` with shared storage' for multi-instance deploys, which means several processes writing one file (and on NFS/EFS, SQLite's advisory locking is unreliable outright). I verified the error propagation: neither `store.consume` at captcha.ts:118 nor `consumeCaptcha` at captcha.ts:151 is wrapped, so a SQLITE_BUSY throw escapes the application's form action. I also confirmed the cited precedent precisely — grepping every `new Database(` in the repo returns exactly two file-backed opens, and support/src/db.server.ts:69 is the only one that runs `PRAGMA journal_mode = WAL`, making SqliteNonceStore the lone sqlite store in the project without it. BUG is the right severity: this is an availability/robustness defect under concurrency, not a boundary bypass. My only residual uncertainty is whether bun:sqlite sets an undocumented internal busy_timeout; the WAL half is certain regardless, and the absence of any pragma is the substance of the finding."
  },
  {
    "findingId": "F7",
    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "reasoning": "Confirmed on all three legs. (1) `new Database(path, { create: true })` at L28 creates the file but not its parent directories — SQLite returns SQLITE_CANTOPEN for a missing directory — and this repo states it outright: support/src/db.server.ts:67 carries the comment 'bun:sqlite won't create the parent directory.' immediately above its own `mkdirSync`. (2) The cited precedents are real: queue.ts:492 is `mkdirSync(path.dirname(path.resolve(file)), { recursive: true })` before constructing the SQL handle, and cache-storage.ts:301 does `mkdirSync(this.directory, { recursive: true })`; SqliteNonceStore does neither. (3) The non-cached-failure amplification is real: config.ts:73-83 assigns `g[GLOBAL_KEY] = runtime` only after `createStore(resolved)` returns, so a throwing constructor leaves the global unset and the construction is retried — and rethrown — on every subsequent request. I verified the boot-vs-request timing claim too: grepping `getCaptchaRuntime` shows the only non-captcha.ts caller is Mochi.ts:672, guarded by `protectionEnabled && options.protection`, and `resolveCaptchaOptions` is reachable only through `getCaptchaRuntime`. So with protection off, the runtime (and the store) is built lazily on the first `mintCaptcha` during a page render, and a bad `storePath` yields a clean startup followed by an unending stream of 500s. The default path is safe — Mochi.ts:602 mkdirs outDir subdirectories, so `.mochi/` exists — which correctly scopes this to a custom `storePath` with a missing parent (e.g. `data/nonces.sqlite`), a configuration the support app exposes directly via its `CAPTCHA_NONCE_DB` env var. BUG severity is right; both recommendations (mkdir in the constructor, validate the store eagerly at boot) follow existing in-repo patterns."
  },
  {
    "findingId": "F8",
    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "reasoning": "I walked each sub-claim through the code. resolveCaptchaOptions bounds-checks `bits` (L33-35), `driftAllowanceMs` (L44-46) and `solveBudgetMs` (L50-52) but not `minAgeMs`, `maxAgeMs`, `store` or `storePath`, and because every NaN comparison is false the single remaining guard at L38 (`minAgeMs >= maxAgeMs`) is porous in both directions. (1) `maxAgeMs: NaN`: `2000 >= NaN` is false so it passes; at verify `limitMs = NaN + 30_000 = NaN`, the L96 guard is also bypassed (`2000 >= NaN` is false, and `Number.isFinite(2000)` holds), and the expiry test `ageMs > limitMs` is then always false — token expiry is silently off. `expiresAt = iat + NaN = NaN`, so MemoryNonceStore's `exp < now` prune never fires and the map grows without bound, while SqliteNonceStore binds a non-finite value against `expires_at INTEGER NOT NULL` and throws (SQLite stores NaN as NULL). (2) `minAgeMs: NaN` likewise clears L38 and surfaces only at request time as the misattributed filter error at captcha.ts:97 — which is the same defect F4 covers from the other file. (3) `store` is unvalidated and createStore's fallthrough at L92 returns it verbatim, so a bogus literal becomes a request-time `store.consume is not a function` TypeError rather than a boot error; TypeScript blocks this for typed callers, so it needs JS or a cast, and it does fail closed. The cited real-world evidence is accurate: support/src/index.ts:55-58 hand-rolls `Number(env) || DEFAULT`, an explicit `Number.isFinite(...)` ternary, and a literal-union narrowing — exactly the guards the framework omits — and that app's own hole is real too, since `Number('')` is `0` and finite, so `CAPTCHA_MIN_AGE_MS=\"\"` drops the production support form's timing floor to zero. BUG is correct: the security control (expiry) genuinely disappears, but only via an application passing a non-finite value, which `Number(process.env.X)` on an unset variable makes a plausible mistake rather than an attacker-reachable one."
  },
  {
    "findingId": "F9",
    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "reasoning": "Verified against source and git history. types.ts:9 reads 'Proof-of-work difficulty in leading zero bits. Default: 16.' while config.ts:27 defines `DEFAULT_CAPTCHA_BITS = 19`; packages/mochi/src/types.ts:705 repeats the stale value ('Default: 16 bits, a 2s floor, a 15-minute expiry, and an in-memory nonce store'), and 150-captcha.md correctly states 19 in both its Options table (L163) and its Props table (L98). Git confirms the drift: `git log -S 'Default: 16.'` on captcha/types.ts points at e7335002, the original captcha commit, and `git show 5028c6b6^:…/config.ts` shows the pre-change code was `const bits = o.bits ?? 16;` — so commit 5028c6b6 raised the default to 19 and left the JSDoc and both widget prop defaults behind. MochiCaptcha.svelte:12 and MochiCaptchaAuto.svelte:11 are still `bits = 16`. The functional consequence the finding draws is sound: a developer writing `<MochiCaptcha token={captcha.token} />` instead of spreading `{...captcha}` gets a widget that brute-forces for 16 leading zeros, sets `verified = true`, submits, and is rejected at captcha.ts:111 roughly 7 times in 8 (a 16-bit solution has a 1/8 chance of also clearing 19), collapsing into the deliberately generic 'Verification failed' message with the real cause only on the `captcha:verify` event. I checked the framework's own call sites are unaffected — templates/ProtectionShell/ProtectionShell.svelte passes `{bits}` explicitly from the minted result. Note the mismatch fails closed, not open (the widget under-solves and is refused), so this is a documentation/consistency footgun rather than a security weakness; BUG and the finding's 'high' confidence are both right, and the recommendation to reference DEFAULT_CAPTCHA_BITS instead of a literal is the correct fix."
  }
]
```

**Summary:** all nine findings are true positives — the code behaves exactly as each describes, and I verified every cited line, test, and in-repo precedent (including the `git show 5028c6b6^` history for F9 and Bun's own SQLite docs for F6). Two severity adjustments: **F2** MEDIUM→BUG (the `bits` value is app-set, not attacker-reachable, and the misconfiguration simultaneously blocks all legitimate submissions) and **F5** MEDIUM→BUG (every map entry costs the attacker a full 2^19 proof-of-work, so the DoS economics run backwards; the unbounded growth is the real half). **F1** keeps MEDIUM — it is precisely the constant-AAD, unscoped-capability-token pattern the project's own threat model flags, and `minBits` is genuinely undocumented in `packages/docs/`. The strongest corroboration for **F3** was finding the handled mirror case: `clearance.ts:39` tolerates negative age and `protection.test.ts:87` tests it, while the `minAgeMs: 0` captcha path does neither.
